<!--
  Study Mode. A closed book with a marker in it — the deck you come back to.

  The three shortcut icons carry a literal colour and no android:tint, and both
  halves of that are deliberate.

  No tint, because a theme attribute would not resolve here. A shortcut icon is
  drawn by the launcher's process out of this app's resources but under the
  LAUNCHER's theme, so ?attr/colorControlNormal is a reference to something that
  is not there — it resolves to nothing or throws, and either way the menu entry
  comes up blank on a device nobody tested.

  The colour is #865c28, which is navStudy in the Retro scheme (THEMES in
  src/App.jsx) — the exact amber of the Study Mode button the shortcut lands on.
  Retro rather than Future because a resource cannot follow the colour scheme the
  way the launcher icon does (that needs a whole alias per scheme, see
  LauncherIconPlugin, and shortcuts get no such mechanism), and Retro is what the
  manifest makes the default. These three stops are also mid-tone by
  construction, which is what lets one file read on both a light and a dark
  launcher background.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
